WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.098
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:13.082 --> 00:00:14.115
Hi, I'm Una,

00:00:14.115 --> 00:00:18.307
a developer advocate on the Chrome team
focusing on CSS and WebView-x.

00:00:18.307 --> 00:00:21.548
Thank you for joining me today,
I am super excited to get started

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:25.899
and talk about some magical lines of CSS
that do some serious heavy lifting,

00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:28.543
and let you build 
robust modern layouts.

00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:31.543
Before we dive in to that,
there are a couple of key terms

00:00:31.543 --> 00:00:33.780
that will help you 
in your styling journeys,

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.433
and are super good to know about
as we walk through these techniques.

00:00:37.433 --> 00:00:39.299
Most of the items I'm mentioning today

00:00:39.299 --> 00:00:42.659
are used in conjunction 
with Grid or Flexbox layout

00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:47.360
and you can denote those as <i>display: grid</i>
or <i>display: flex</i> on the parent element.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.124
The first key terms are <i>fr</i> and <i>auto</i>,
these are used with grid layouts

00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:54.872
to denote fractional units of space, 
that's <i>fr</i>

00:00:54.872 --> 00:00:56.915
or automatic space, that's <i>auto</i>

00:00:56.915 --> 00:01:00.797
based on the minimum content size
of the items within that element.

00:01:00.797 --> 00:01:03.872
For grid layout we also have
the <i>minmax()</i> function,

00:01:03.872 --> 00:01:07.653
which lets us set a minimum 
and maximum value with our layout bounds

00:01:07.653 --> 00:01:10.553
to enable responsive design 
without media queries.

00:01:10.553 --> 00:01:13.433
We also have separate <i>min() max() </i>
and <i>clamp()</i> functions

00:01:13.433 --> 00:01:17.119
available in some browsers 
that bring logic to CSS.

00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:19.318
The browser determines 
which value to choose

00:01:19.318 --> 00:01:21.787
based on the function 
provided for <i>min()</i> and <i>max(),</i>

00:01:21.787 --> 00:01:24.252
and for <i>clamp()</i>, 
we set both a <i>min()</i> and <i>max()</i>

00:01:24.252 --> 00:01:26.419
with a relative value in between.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:29.734
We'll definitely be covering these 
within the video with the demo.

00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:33.466
And within these layouts 
we can also use <i>auto-fit</i> or <i>auto-fill</i>

00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:36.915
to automatically place child elements
into a parent grid.

00:01:36.915 --> 00:01:41.598
This is another tool for dynamic 
responsive layouts without media queries.

00:01:41.598 --> 00:01:44.746
We're going to be going over 
all of those terms and more

00:01:44.746 --> 00:01:46.348
with demos galore.

00:01:46.668 --> 00:01:49.159
I'm a really big nerd, I don't know 
why I made that rhyme

00:01:49.159 --> 00:01:50.986
and put that in this video, but I did,

00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:55.094
so let's just dive into these demos 
and 1 line layouts.

00:01:55.094 --> 00:02:00.260
I made a handy little site called 
1 line layouts at <i>1linelayouts.glitch.me</i>

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.627
so that you can follow along 
or play on your own

00:02:02.627 --> 00:02:05.979
and have a reference for the power 
that CSS can bring to your layouts.

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:08.476
For our first single line layout,

00:02:08.476 --> 00:02:13.010
let's solve the biggest mystery in all 
the CSS land: centering things.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:17.075
I want you to know that's easier
than you think with place items centered.

00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:19.910
I call this the 
"definitely centered layout."

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.170
What we need to do is first specify
the layout method,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.752
which is <i>display: grid</i> here,

00:02:24.752 --> 00:02:27.298
and then we are going to write 
<i>place-items: center</i>

00:02:27.298 --> 00:02:29.674
this is that one magical line of code,

00:02:29.674 --> 00:02:32.073
and I have these highlighted 
under the titles here.

00:02:32.073 --> 00:02:35.086
So what happens here is 
no matter what you put in here,

00:02:35.086 --> 00:02:38.670
it is going to stay centered 
to that parent element.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.976
So if we look at the HTML, 
we have this parent,

00:02:41.976 --> 00:02:43.853
and it's just getting 
a background in blue,

00:02:43.853 --> 00:02:46.250
and then this child 
with a coral background,

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.881
and it's content editable
so we can actually just type in here,

00:02:48.881 --> 00:02:51.683
we can have vertical content, "hello world,"

00:02:51.683 --> 00:02:57.632
and even as I'm typing, this child element
it staying centered within the parent box.

00:02:57.632 --> 00:03:00.217
So I think that this is 
a really cool technique,

00:03:00.217 --> 00:03:03.880
<i>place-item:center</i>
will solve all of your centered dilemmas.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.580
Next we have the Deconstructed Pancake.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.547
This is something that we see 
all the time on marketing sites,

00:03:09.547 --> 00:03:11.676
you see this row of three items,

00:03:11.676 --> 00:03:14.495
and usually on mobile 
we'll want that to be stacked,

00:03:14.495 --> 00:03:18.811
wich is why I called this 
the pancake stack but deconstructed,

00:03:18.811 --> 00:03:22.126
because as you increase
the size of the viewport,

00:03:22.126 --> 00:03:25.508
those items will start spanning 
into the same line,

00:03:25.508 --> 00:03:28.244
so it starts when they're all 
on top of each other,

00:03:28.244 --> 00:03:32.276
and then they start to deconstruct
as you increase the size of the viewport.

00:03:32.276 --> 00:03:34.891
So the way that we're going 
to be doing this one

00:03:34.891 --> 00:03:37.107
is using the shorthand for Flexbox,

00:03:37.107 --> 00:03:41.295
<i>flex: 0 1 &lt;baseWidth&gt;</i>
is what we will be using for this look

00:03:41.295 --> 00:03:43.572
that you're seeing right now 
without the stretching.

00:03:43.572 --> 00:03:47.090
But if we did want it to stretch, 
we would set that to <i>flex: 1 1</i>

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:48.799
and then the flex basis.

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:54.203
And the reason why we do this is because 
the flex shorthand stands for flex grow,

00:03:54.203 --> 00:03:56.553
flex shrink, and then flex basis.

00:03:56.553 --> 00:04:01.252
So again, here we have <i>flex: 0 1 150px</i>, 
that is the basis here

00:04:01.252 --> 00:04:05.702
so as we increase the size
it is not going to be growing much.

00:04:05.702 --> 00:04:09.067
Also, when we decrease it, it's going 
to be staying within that 150px size.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:11.237
When we do change that to 1,

00:04:11.237 --> 00:04:12.934
I'm just gonna delete
this line of code here,

00:04:12.934 --> 00:04:15.468
now we see that 
this is going to be stretching,

00:04:15.468 --> 00:04:19.360
so here you can see that it stretches
to fill the space, even as it wraps,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.730
and as I increase it to be even larger 
of a viewport size,

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:25.098
these items are going to be 
filling that space.

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:27.036
So this is the Deconstructed Pancake,

00:04:27.036 --> 00:04:29.845
a really common technique
that we see on marketing sites.

00:04:29.845 --> 00:04:33.351
This is usually is gonna be like an image 
with some text about the product,

00:04:33.351 --> 00:04:35.945
and you can write it 
by using the flex shorthand.

00:04:36.705 --> 00:04:39.190
I like to call this one Sidebar Says,

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:43.228
and it takes advantage of that 
<i>minmax()</i> function for grid layouts.

00:04:43.228 --> 00:04:47.186
So that is the line here, we have 
<i>grid-template-columns: minmax()</i>

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:49.822
and then <i>&lt;min&gt;</i> and <i>&lt;max&gt;</i> value.

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.932
That's pretty straightforward 
with <i>minmax().</i>

00:04:51.932 --> 00:04:53.427
But what this is doing

00:04:53.427 --> 00:04:56.086
is essentially as we are increasing 
the size of the viewport,

00:04:56.086 --> 00:04:59.739
it's going to be taking that 25% size, 
that max size,

00:04:59.739 --> 00:05:04.262
and as we decrease it, 
it hits this point where it's 150px,

00:05:04.262 --> 00:05:07.673
and where 25% is smaller than 150px,

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:10.641
so it's going to clamp it 
at that minimum size.

00:05:10.641 --> 00:05:14.753
So it's going to be increasing 
when it can take up that viewport space,

00:05:14.753 --> 00:05:17.614
but if we have content in here 
that we don't want squeezed,

00:05:17.614 --> 00:05:21.765
or we want it to stop being 25%, 
and at a minimum be 150px,

00:05:21.765 --> 00:05:23.585
or whatever value we set here,

00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:27.135
then that it's exactly what <i>minmax()</i>
is doing for us.

00:05:27.135 --> 00:05:29.635
And in the grid template columns 
here we are writing

00:05:29.635 --> 00:05:32.472
<i>grid-template-columns: minmax()</i>
that base value,

00:05:32.472 --> 00:05:35.119
and then the relative value, so 25%,

00:05:35.119 --> 00:05:37.502
and that second element is getting <i>1fr</i>.

00:05:37.502 --> 00:05:39.768
So if we look at the HTML, 
we have these two elements,

00:05:39.768 --> 00:05:43.148
this yellow side bar section, 
and then this purple content section,

00:05:43.148 --> 00:05:46.706
and they are taking up the units 
of space that we are specifying.

00:05:46.706 --> 00:05:49.321
In this specific case, 
we can actually even set this to auto,

00:05:49.321 --> 00:05:51.530
and it would be looking the same,

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.544
because we are only setting the size
to that first element.

00:05:54.544 --> 00:05:58.079
We're gonna go over auto versus 1fr 
in the next example.

00:05:58.079 --> 00:06:02.678
But I think that is this pretty neat, 
a great way to set a minimum size,

00:06:02.678 --> 00:06:05.961
but then let your elements stretch 
on larger viewports

00:06:05.961 --> 00:06:09.088
to fit those layouts a little bit better,

00:06:09.088 --> 00:06:11.659
based on how your user 
is seeing your website.

00:06:12.299 --> 00:06:14.616
Next we have the Pancake Stack,

00:06:14.616 --> 00:06:16.563
unlike the Deconstructed Pancake,

00:06:16.563 --> 00:06:20.309
this one do not shift 
when the screen changes sizes.

00:06:20.309 --> 00:06:23.978
This is a very common layout 
that we see for both websites

00:06:23.978 --> 00:06:27.420
and applications across mobile 
and desktop functions.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:29.423
So it looks like this,

00:06:29.423 --> 00:06:31.523
as we are increasing 
and decreasing the size

00:06:31.523 --> 00:06:33.808
this content is not changing,

00:06:33.808 --> 00:06:35.838
and what we are doing to create this,

00:06:35.838 --> 00:06:41.386
is we are writing the <i>grid-template-rows:</i>
to be <i>auto 1fr</i> and <i>auto</i>.

00:06:41.386 --> 00:06:45.853
So what we are doing is essentially 
telling the first and last row

00:06:45.853 --> 00:06:49.898
to take up the space 
that the internal elements allow.

00:06:49.898 --> 00:06:53.174
So if this header was two lines,
then it's going to increasingly

00:06:53.174 --> 00:06:56.859
take up more space 
within this vertical layout.

00:06:57.299 --> 00:07:00.949
With this <i>auto</i> section
I could have more content here,

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:04.083
but it's not going to increase the size,
because what we're doing

00:07:04.083 --> 00:07:10.147
is setting the auto placed row first
to take up that specific size

00:07:10.147 --> 00:07:11.778
based on the content within it,

00:07:11.778 --> 00:07:15.094
and then the remaining space,
is the remaining fractional unit.

00:07:15.094 --> 00:07:17.962
So as we increase this also vertically,

00:07:17.962 --> 00:07:22.076
we can see that the first 
and last row are not growing.

00:07:22.076 --> 00:07:24.862
They're still only gonna be taking
the size that they need,

00:07:24.862 --> 00:07:27.401
but if I decrease the horizontal space,

00:07:27.401 --> 00:07:29.598
as Footer Content 
moves on to the next line,

00:07:29.598 --> 00:07:32.719
it is going to take space up 
within that layout.

00:07:32.719 --> 00:07:36.941
So with <i>grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto</i>
you can create the pancake stack.

00:07:36.941 --> 00:07:39.626
You could even have 
an application toolbar down there.

00:07:39.626 --> 00:07:42.690
Again, this is the one 
that we see really commonly,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.171
and <i>grid-template-rows</i>
is a great one to know.

00:07:45.691 --> 00:07:48.892
Another very common layout
is the Holy Grail layout.

00:07:48.892 --> 00:07:50.572
Does this look familiar?

00:07:50.572 --> 00:07:52.629
I think we've all have seen
a website or two

00:07:52.629 --> 00:07:56.104
that looks exactly like this at some point
on our web journey.

00:07:56.104 --> 00:08:00.638
We've got your Header, your Footer,
your Left Sidebar over here,

00:08:00.638 --> 00:08:03.170
your Main Content, 
and then a Right Sidebar.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:08.118
And we can write all of this
in one line of code using <i>grid-template</i>.

00:08:08.118 --> 00:08:12.502
So <i>grid-template</i> allows us to write
the <i>grid-template-rows</i>

00:08:12.502 --> 00:08:16.082
and <i>grid-template-columns</i>
at the same time.

00:08:16.082 --> 00:08:18.699
So that's pretty neat. 
It's also super responsive.

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:21.648
Again, you see this auto content taking up

00:08:21.648 --> 00:08:24.516
what we are specifying here internally,

00:08:24.516 --> 00:08:27.349
and then this Main Content 
taking up the <i>1fr</i>,

00:08:27.349 --> 00:08:30.615
and so what we are specifying
in here is <i>grid-template</i>

00:08:30.615 --> 00:08:35.262
with <i>auto 1fr</i> for this middle space,
and then <i>auto</i> for the footer,

00:08:35.262 --> 00:08:38.995
and then we've got our columns 
of <i>auto 1fr</i> and <i>auto</i>.

00:08:38.995 --> 00:08:41.711
So you get the whole Holy Grail layout

00:08:41.711 --> 00:08:45.460
with <i>grid-template:</i>
and <i>auto 1fr auto / auto 1fr auto</i>.

00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:49.409
This slash is what denotes 
our rows versus columns

00:08:49.409 --> 00:08:51.777
when we are writing the <i>grid-template</i>.

00:08:51.777 --> 00:08:54.359
So I think that this one is great to know,

00:08:54.359 --> 00:08:56.777
<i>grid-template</i> is something 
that I use all the time.

00:08:56.777 --> 00:08:59.291
This is not like exactly one line, I mean,

00:08:59.291 --> 00:09:01.340
all of these you have 
a little bit more to add,

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.174
but with this when you're already 
in <i>grid-template</i>,

00:09:04.174 --> 00:09:10.794
you can then specify <i>grid-columns</i>
and <i>grid-rows</i> for each of these elements.

00:09:10.794 --> 00:09:15.055
So here I'm specifying what grid column
I'm placing the Header in.

00:09:15.055 --> 00:09:20.143
I'm going all the way across all three, 
so <i>1 / 4</i> to get those grid tracks

00:09:20.143 --> 00:09:22.055
from the first track to the last track,

00:09:22.055 --> 00:09:26.833
and then the left side we have it 
going inside the first, so from 1 to 2,

00:09:26.833 --> 00:09:30.116
this main section going from 2 to 3,

00:09:30.116 --> 00:09:32.153
so that's gonna be taking up 
the middle section.

00:09:32.153 --> 00:09:34.185
And then the right side is from 3 to 4,

00:09:34.185 --> 00:09:37.701
so that's going all the way to the end
of this grid that we created.

00:09:37.701 --> 00:09:40.503
And then for that footer it's going
all the way across just like the header.

00:09:40.503 --> 00:09:43.881
So you do specify where items are placed,

00:09:43.881 --> 00:09:46.670
when it is a little bit more 
of a complex UI,

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.676
and here if we look at the HTML,

00:09:48.676 --> 00:09:53.271
I have this parent, then I have header,
then I have left side bar,

00:09:53.271 --> 00:09:58.637
then I have the main section, 
right section, and then the footer.

00:09:58.637 --> 00:10:04.073
So, you can write all of your layouts
in one line using <i>grid-template</i> property.

00:10:04.593 --> 00:10:08.679
Next we have another classic, 
the 12-By or 12-Span Grid.

00:10:08.679 --> 00:10:13.025
You can quickly write grids in CSS 
using the repeat grid function.

00:10:13.025 --> 00:10:17.564
And here we're setting a repeat 
of 12 columns, so that looks like this:

00:10:17.564 --> 00:10:22.591
<i>grid-template-columns: repeat</i> the number 
that we want to repeat, and then <i>1fr</i>.

00:10:22.591 --> 00:10:24.874
So this would be the same as writing

00:10:24.874 --> 00:10:28.856
<i>grid-template-columns:
1fr 1fr 1fr...</i> 12 times,

00:10:28.856 --> 00:10:30.705
but because I don't want to do that,

00:10:30.705 --> 00:10:33.522
I'm just gonna write <i>repeat 12</i>
and then <i>1fr,</i>

00:10:33.522 --> 00:10:36.271
and then we have a 12-By grid.

00:10:36.271 --> 00:10:40.505
So now we can place our items 
within this grid however we want.

00:10:40.505 --> 00:10:43.457
If we want it to span
all the way across the 12 columns,

00:10:43.457 --> 00:10:47.578
we would use this <i>span-12</i> element--
I just gave it a <i>span-12</i> class--

00:10:47.578 --> 00:10:50.149
and have it going from 1 
to all the way to 13,

00:10:50.149 --> 00:10:51.863
so it's taking up the full space

00:10:51.863 --> 00:10:55.231
of the 12 columns ending 
at the grid track of 13,

00:10:55.231 --> 00:10:58.634
which would be the end 
of that 12th column line.

00:10:58.634 --> 00:11:04.435
For the <i>span-6</i>, this is going from 1 to 7,
for <i>span-4</i>, this is going from 4 to 9.

00:11:04.435 --> 00:11:05.742
It can go anywhere.

00:11:05.742 --> 00:11:09.166
I could start this at the first line 
and make it go all the way to 9.

00:11:09.166 --> 00:11:11.985
I could have this go to 5 
and have it still <i>span-4,</i>

00:11:11.985 --> 00:11:16.295
but the cool thing is you can just place
this wherever you want inside of your UI.

00:11:16.295 --> 00:11:20.163
So I fI wanted to have this start
from 6 and go to 10, I could do that.

00:11:20.163 --> 00:11:23.526
And then we have this <i>span-2</i>,
and this is just going from 3 to 5,

00:11:25.347 --> 00:11:28.938
And then a peep at the HTML, 
we just have this parent element,

00:11:28.938 --> 00:11:31.746
and then the inside of here, 
we're just giving it classes

00:11:31.746 --> 00:11:36.799
based on those span elements that we were
just adjusting within that grid template.

00:11:36.799 --> 00:11:40.502
So the repeat function is very, 
very useful when you don't want

00:11:40.502 --> 00:11:44.200
to keep typing out <i>1fr auto</i>
multiple times,

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.937
it just lets you
quickly write information.

00:11:47.393 --> 00:11:51.175
We are going to expand 
on the repeat function for number seven.

00:11:51.175 --> 00:11:54.117
And this technique is super cool, 
super useful,

00:11:54.117 --> 00:11:56.165
if you take away anything from this video,

00:11:56.165 --> 00:11:59.031
I think this would be a great one
to keep in your repertoire.

00:11:59.031 --> 00:12:03.973
I like to call this the RAM technique, 
which stands for Repeat Auto and Minmax.

00:12:03.973 --> 00:12:06.130
And the line of code here looks like this.

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:10.237
You have the <i>grid-template-columns:
repeat(auto-fit </i> or <i>auto-fill,</i>

00:12:10.237 --> 00:12:15.535
<i>minmax</i> (the base value,
and then <i>1fr</i>)) for a fractional unit.

00:12:15.535 --> 00:12:18.270
And here we have that base value as 150px,

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.117
I'll show you exactly what's going on.

00:12:20.117 --> 00:12:22.784
So as we increase the size here,

00:12:22.784 --> 00:12:27.535
these elements are going to fit 
to take up the space, these four boxes,

00:12:27.535 --> 00:12:31.928
as I decrease it, they're going to hit 
this 150px base value,

00:12:31.928 --> 00:12:35.365
and then they're going to wrap
onto the next line.

00:12:35.365 --> 00:12:38.280
But here we have them <i>auto-fitting</i>,

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:42.496
so they're going to be spanning 
to take up as much space as they can.

00:12:42.496 --> 00:12:45.696
There's some really cool 
algorithms at play here.

00:12:45.696 --> 00:12:50.678
Now if we change this to <i>auto-fill</i>, 
this will look a little bit different,

00:12:50.678 --> 00:12:52.595
so let me just update that to <i>auto-fill</i>.

00:12:52.595 --> 00:12:55.244
Here now as I increase the size,

00:12:55.244 --> 00:12:57.960
it's not going to be spanning
and stretching

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.333
to take up the remainder of the space.

00:13:00.333 --> 00:13:04.639
It's actually going to be suing that 150px
as a baseline and stay within that.

00:13:04.639 --> 00:13:07.020
At smaller sizes,
there's no difference here,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.992
but you really see the difference
at larger sizes

00:13:09.992 --> 00:13:12.358
as you have additional space.

00:13:12.358 --> 00:13:16.925
And I use this technique on the page 
that you're looking at here itself,

00:13:16.925 --> 00:13:22.631
this <i>auto-fill</i>, so that these two segments
would stretch and shrink

00:13:22.631 --> 00:13:26.817
but not exceed a specific space 
that I wanted them to,

00:13:26.817 --> 00:13:28.309
and then I have them centered.

00:13:28.309 --> 00:13:33.346
So you can center this within the parent 
as it spreads to a larger size.

00:13:33.346 --> 00:13:36.853
That's always an option, 
and a really great technique to know.

00:13:36.853 --> 00:13:42.400
Remember R-A-M, RAM, Repeat, 
Auto-fill or fit, and then Minmax,

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.055
and you can get these responsive boxes,
you can use these for images,

00:13:46.055 --> 00:13:47.834
you can use them for a lot of things.

00:13:47.834 --> 00:13:50.636
It's something that we see 
all the time for cards,

00:13:50.636 --> 00:13:56.471
and this is a great use of all of these
fancy new grid capabilities in CSS.

00:13:57.181 --> 00:14:00.382
For our next layout, 
we are heading back to Flexbox land,

00:14:00.382 --> 00:14:03.130
I wanted to include this one 
because I just find it so useful

00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:04.895
and I use this all the time.

00:14:04.895 --> 00:14:08.546
I call it the Line Up, why?
I don't know. I'm tired.

00:14:08.546 --> 00:14:11.174
If you have a better name,
please let me know, leave a comment.

00:14:11.174 --> 00:14:13.660
But the main thing that I wanted 
to demonstrate here

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:16.643
is <i>justify-content</i> for placement of items.

00:14:16.643 --> 00:14:18.561
And here specifically, I wanted to highlight

00:14:18.561 --> 00:14:22.529
<i>justify-content: space-between</i>
to place items at their edges.

00:14:22.529 --> 00:14:25.376
And so in this example 
we have these three cards,

00:14:25.376 --> 00:14:28.642
and you can see that 
as I'm stretching or shrinking

00:14:28.642 --> 00:14:31.326
the size of this viewport element,

00:14:31.326 --> 00:14:34.293
they are maintaining 
the same height as each other.

00:14:34.293 --> 00:14:39.644
And in fact we are having them 
fit to the top and bottom,

00:14:39.644 --> 00:14:41.473
and this interior content,

00:14:41.473 --> 00:14:45.104
so this description, like, 
I can keep typing here,

00:14:45.104 --> 00:14:49.849
this is then going to be centered 
within the remaining space.

00:14:49.849 --> 00:14:54.870
And so the reason that this is happening 
is because for these cards

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.585
we're giving them this 
<i>flex-direction: column</i>

00:14:57.585 --> 00:14:59.620
this <i>display: flex</i>, the Flexbox mode,

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.951
and then we are 
<i>justify-content: space-between</i>.

00:15:02.951 --> 00:15:06.451
So because this is a column 
for the <i>flex-direction</i>,

00:15:06.451 --> 00:15:09.203
the <i>space-between</i>
is going to be right here

00:15:09.203 --> 00:15:11.383
in between these three elements,

00:15:11.383 --> 00:15:14.117
this little box here, 
the description and the title.

00:15:14.117 --> 00:15:19.214
And so here as I'm having additional space
added or removed vertically,

00:15:19.214 --> 00:15:20.735
they are centering themselves,

00:15:20.735 --> 00:15:24.197
and this just makes
for a much neater layout.

00:15:24.197 --> 00:15:26.839
I've used this a lot here 
without that justification,

00:15:26.839 --> 00:15:28.162
it looks a lot more messy,

00:15:28.162 --> 00:15:30.879
but because of the stretching here, 
because of Flexbox,

00:15:30.879 --> 00:15:32.780
because this 
<i>justify-content: space-between</i>,

00:15:32.780 --> 00:15:36.234
I use this all the time, 
and I think it's really important to know

00:15:36.234 --> 00:15:39.325
that with Flexbox 
you can change the direction

00:15:39.325 --> 00:15:41.875
and you can justify 
your content in unique ways.

00:15:41.875 --> 00:15:45.458
It doesn't have to be just centered, 
you can also do <i>center</i>,

00:15:45.458 --> 00:15:49.526
you can do <i>space-around</i> as well, 
or <i>space-evenly</i>.

00:15:49.526 --> 00:15:53.639
But in this case I think that the best way
to justify is <i>space-between</i>

00:15:53.639 --> 00:15:56.173
because this way we are ensuring

00:15:56.173 --> 00:16:00.723
that the first and last element
here in our layout,

00:16:00.723 --> 00:16:04.517
which is going to be this <i>h3</i>, 
and this whole visual box,

00:16:04.517 --> 00:16:07.849
remain flush to the top
and bottom of our cards.

00:16:08.649 --> 00:16:10.598
Here's where we get into some techniques

00:16:10.598 --> 00:16:12.971
with a little less 
current browser support.

00:16:12.971 --> 00:16:17.178
I like to call this one Clamping My Style,
and it's a really neat trick.

00:16:17.178 --> 00:16:21.638
Remember at the beginning of this video, 
we talked about <i>min() max()</i> and <i>clamp()</i>.

00:16:21.638 --> 00:16:25.903
Well, here's where it can come into play 
for layouts and element style.

00:16:25.903 --> 00:16:29.717
Here I'm specifying the width 
using this <i>clamp()</i> function,

00:16:29.717 --> 00:16:34.887
and I'm setting <i>clamp(23ch, 50%, 46ch)</i>.

00:16:34.887 --> 00:16:36.050
What does this do?

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:38.115
Let me show you exactly 
what's going on here.

00:16:38.115 --> 00:16:42.965
As I increase the size of this element, 
the parent container here,

00:16:42.965 --> 00:16:46.432
it is going to increase
the size of this card,

00:16:46.432 --> 00:16:48.730
and as I decrease it, it decreases it.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:50.333
But what we are setting here

00:16:50.333 --> 00:16:54.196
is a minimum and maximum size
for it to clamp to

00:16:54.196 --> 00:16:57.560
as it reaches that 50% size.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:00.755
This card wants to be at 50% width,

00:17:00.755 --> 00:17:04.512
but if that 50% means
that it's bigger than 46ch,

00:17:04.512 --> 00:17:10.025
which stands for 46 characters, 
then it's going to stop getting bigger.

00:17:10.025 --> 00:17:12.943
The same thing happens 
with the smaller viewport size.

00:17:12.943 --> 00:17:18.307
It's going to stop decreasing in size 
when 50% of it's parent size

00:17:18.307 --> 00:17:22.839
means that it is smaller than 23ch, 
which is 23 characters.

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:24.806
And these specific character units

00:17:24.806 --> 00:17:28.327
can be used to make legibility 
a little bit easier.

00:17:28.327 --> 00:17:31.688
So in this case, I don't want this card
to get any smaller than that

00:17:31.688 --> 00:17:33.771
because then the paragraph 
gets harder to read,

00:17:33.771 --> 00:17:36.986
and the same thing with getting bigger 
than 46 characters,

00:17:36.986 --> 00:17:39.172
then it gets too long and hard to read.

00:17:39.172 --> 00:17:44.936
So you can use width: <i>clamp(minimum size,
the actual size, and the max size)</i>

00:17:44.936 --> 00:17:49.135
to create some really cool 
responsiveness within this element itself.

00:17:49.135 --> 00:17:53.451
And you can also use clamp for font size,
that's a really great use case too.

00:17:53.451 --> 00:17:56.383
You can have this responsive 
flexible typography,

00:17:56.383 --> 00:18:01.034
you can have like <i>clamp(1.5rems</i>,
and then a--

00:18:02.034 --> 00:18:08.011
say <i>10vw</i> for the viewport width, 
and then <i>3rems</i> as the biggest size,

00:18:08.011 --> 00:18:14.163
and that way as you resize your window,
you're going to be having a minimum value

00:18:14.163 --> 00:18:16.730
of 1.5 rems, maximum value of 3 rems,

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:21.585
and then you're gonna have that grow 
and shrink into that remaining space.

00:18:21.585 --> 00:18:26.110
So it's pretty cool to see this 
actually working in a browser.

00:18:26.110 --> 00:18:28.959
Again, this doesn't have 
full browser supports,

00:18:28.959 --> 00:18:31.760
but it is a really great technique.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:34.042
So if you're using this,
make sure that you have fallbacks

00:18:34.042 --> 00:18:35.576
and do your browser testing.

00:18:36.026 --> 00:18:40.226
And finally, we are at the end, 
and this is the last layout tool,

00:18:40.226 --> 00:18:43.275
and this layout tool 
is the most experimental of the bunch.

00:18:43.275 --> 00:18:47.160
This was recently introduced 
to Chrome Canary and Chrome 84+,

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:49.257
and there is an active effort 
from Firefox

00:18:49.257 --> 00:18:51.390
in getting this implemented in browser,

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.389
but it is currently not
in any stable browsers

00:18:54.389 --> 00:18:56.206
at the time of this recording.

00:18:56.206 --> 00:18:59.184
I do want to mention it though, 
because it's such a frequently met problem

00:18:59.184 --> 00:19:01.485
and it's just simply maintaining

00:19:01.485 --> 00:19:05.619
the aspect ratio of an image or a video, 
or of an iframe.

00:19:05.619 --> 00:19:09.621
And so what this is
is respecting the aspect,

00:19:09.621 --> 00:19:11.660
Respect For Aspect, 
that's the name I gave it.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:16.519
And it is the aspect ratio property, 
oh my gosh, I'm gonna be so excited

00:19:16.519 --> 00:19:18.528
when this is implemented in all browsers.

00:19:19.098 --> 00:19:23.734
What this is doing is, 
as I am resizing this parent element,

00:19:23.734 --> 00:19:27.765
the image here, so this is just like, 
I gave it a class of visual

00:19:27.765 --> 00:19:34.165
for this box here that is right here,
this is getting an aspect ratio of 16 to 9

00:19:34.165 --> 00:19:37.247
and no matter how
I increase it or decrease it,

00:19:37.247 --> 00:19:39.430
it is going to keep that aspect ratio.

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:41.512
This is something that is so needed

00:19:41.512 --> 00:19:45.694
for when you are pulling in content
from the CMS or otherwise,

00:19:45.694 --> 00:19:50.076
and you have a specific dimension
that you have that media at,

00:19:50.076 --> 00:19:54.543
and this, this for example, 
in the previous example if I scroll up,

00:19:54.543 --> 00:19:58.127
as I resize here,
since I'm only setting a height,

00:19:58.127 --> 00:20:01.608
and it's just getting the 100% width
of whatever remaining space

00:20:01.608 --> 00:20:05.357
that I'm providing here,
it's actually changing the aspect ratio

00:20:05.357 --> 00:20:08.068
of the whole visual box, 
and that's not what we want.

00:20:08.638 --> 00:20:11.604
That is going to make you force a decision

00:20:11.604 --> 00:20:14.850
on if you want to either
fit the content inside of there,

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.020
so it's smaller and fits within the space,

00:20:17.020 --> 00:20:21.139
or if you stretch it out 
and you have it fill the content,

00:20:21.139 --> 00:20:24.487
and you're only seeing a piece 
of that media, not the full image,

00:20:24.487 --> 00:20:27.304
and that's also gonna cause 
all sorts of problems.

00:20:27.304 --> 00:20:31.702
So having this <i>aspect-ratio</i>
is very exciting,

00:20:31.702 --> 00:20:35.235
you can also set this to a square
if you do one over one,

00:20:35.235 --> 00:20:38.668
you could have this be 1 over 2,
or you have this actually

00:20:38.668 --> 00:20:42.048
longer than it is wide 
within this parent here,

00:20:42.048 --> 00:20:44.433
so that's gonna be maintaining 
that aspect ratio,

00:20:44.433 --> 00:20:46.514
and it's just exciting, 
so I wanted to mention it

00:20:46.514 --> 00:20:48.756
and let you know 
that it's coming down the pipeline.

00:20:49.246 --> 00:20:52.614
And those are 
the 10 really powerful lines of CSS

00:20:52.614 --> 00:20:55.970
that I wanted to talk about
in this 1 line layout video.

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:57.789
I hope that you all learned something new,

00:20:57.789 --> 00:21:02.663
and if you're looking for these demos, 
check out <i>1linelayouts.glitch.me</i>

00:21:02.663 --> 00:21:05.631
Thank you all for watching,
if you want more CSS content

00:21:05.631 --> 00:21:08.010
and to dive deeper into layout techniques,

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:11.646
including all the bells and whistles 
of CSS Grid and Flexbox,

00:21:11.646 --> 00:21:16.030
check out the CSS podcast
that I do with my co-host Adam Argyle.

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:19.908
It is at pod.link/thecsspodcast

00:21:19.908 --> 00:21:22.392
Thank you again,
enjoy the rest of your web day!

00:21:22.392 --> 00:21:26.790
